From eefc9e9fb82f0cac5748fd68066828bca61c3719 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nikola Cvetkovic <ncvetkovic@tenstorrent.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 15:54:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Add POWER_CASE power-experiment scenarios to
 high_power_matmul

Adds the environment-variable controlled ablations that tt-ember's
run_power_cases.py drives. This is the one piece of the workload that exists in
no pushed branch, so run_power_cases.py could not be used against any public
tt-metal tree.

Semantics are reconstructed from the POWER_CASE specification in the
tt-energyprofiler README and verified against the confirmation lines recorded in
Haris's committed Wormhole runs (out_new/*/summary.txt, out_new2/*/summary.txt) --
the strings this prints are character-identical to his for cases 0, 1, 2, 3 and 4.

An 'idle' kernel keeps its full circular-buffer handshake and only skips its real
NoC transfer or FPU work, so no combination deadlocks; verified on p100a for
cases 2, 3 and 4 plus HIGH_POWER_DISABLE_WRITER.

Flags are passed as JIT defines, so changing a scenario recompiles only the
kernels and never the host binary -- matching the behaviour the README describes.

Also extends the grid sweep with 8x7, 9x8 and 10x9. The hardcoded list stopped at
7x7 and then appended only the device maximum, which on Blackhole jumps 49 -> 110
cores with nothing sampled in between. Each addition is dropped by the existing
filter on devices too small to host it, so Wormhole still produces the same 15
grids; Blackhole now sweeps 18.

Based on 1dca802d332, the newest published version of this example
(turkmanovic/tt-metal, branch ncvetkovic/high_power_usage_workload). No other
change is needed: at this commit mm_init and old-style DPRINT still exist and the
bundled tt-umd emits single-value TDP, so tt-ember's scripts run unmodified.
